#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115144 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"}] :question/id #uuid "bb96e3e7-5fc4-58ed-a666-41e990e1b96d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8285" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\n1. The probability that a basketball player scores at least $2$ baskets in a series of $3$ free throws if the probability of scoring a basket on each free throw is $0.55$.\n2. The probability of getting $35$ even numbers on $60$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n3. The probability that a fair spinner with three regions, red, green, and blue, lands on green in $5$ out of $12$ spins." :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, if\n\n- $np > 5$, and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$,\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nthat is,\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p))\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=3$ and $p=0.55$. Therefore,\n$np|= 3 \\cdot 0.55; = 1.65; ≯5.\\cdot$\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=60$ and $p=\\dfrac12$. Therefore,\n$np|= 60 \\cdot 0.5; = 30; > 5✓; n(1 - p)|= 60 \\cdot (1 - 0.5); = 30; > 5.✓$\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n- In situation III, we have $n=12$ and $p=\\dfrac13$. Therefore,\n$np|= 12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}; = 4; ≯5.\\cdot$\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"}] :question/id #uuid "4c4e12c4-1593-500d-873d-863b4a825e91" :question/math-academy-id "e-8286" :question/problem "In a city, $44\\%$ of the people have blood type A. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that if $150$ people are picked at random, then $70$ of them will have blood type A. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7123 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7157 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7190 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7517 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7823 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, if\n\n- $np > 5$, and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$,\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nthat is,\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p))\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=150$ and $p = 0.44$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnp &= 150 \\cdot 0.44 \\\\\n&= 66 \\\\\n&> 5✓ \\\\\nn(1 - p) &= 150 \\cdot (1 - 0.44) \\\\\n&= 84 \\\\\n&> 5.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (150)(0.44) = 66\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (150)(0.44)(1-0.44) = 36.96\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(66, 36.96)$.\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability\n\n$$\nP(X = 70)\n$$\n\nUsing a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X =70) \\approx P(69.5 \\leq Y \\leq 70.5)\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(69.5 \\le Y \\le 70.5) &= P(\\frac{69.5 - 66}{\\sqrt{36.96}} \\le Z \\le \\frac{70.5 - 66}{\\sqrt{36.96}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(0.58 \\le Z \\le 0.74) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 0.74) - P(Z < 0.58) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 0.74) - P(Z \\le 0.58) \\\\\n&= Φ(0.74) - Φ(0.58) \\\\\n&= 0.7704 - 0.7190 \\\\\n&= 0.0514\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 70)\\approx 0.051\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0528$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115146 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "9019ee77-a971-598c-b5b6-4ef903fd5029" :question/math-academy-id "e-8287" :question/problem "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $50$ times. The archer has a $47\\%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye on each shot. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the archer hits the bull's eye more than $29$ times but less than $35$ times. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$$\\, 0.00 \\,$$\\, 0.01 \\,$$\\, 0.02 \\,$$\\, 1.6 \\,$ $\\, 0.9452 \\,$ $\\, 0.9463 \\,$ $\\, 0.9474 \\,$ $\\, 1.7 \\,$ $\\, 0.9554 \\,$ $\\, 0.9564 \\,$ $\\, 0.9573 \\,$ $\\, 1.8 \\,$ $\\, 0.9641 \\,$ $\\, 0.9649 \\,$ $\\, 0.9656 \\,$ |  | $z$$\\, 0.00 \\,$$\\, 0.01 \\,$$\\, 0.02 \\,$$\\, 3.0 \\,$ $\\, 0.9987 \\,$ $\\, 0.9987 \\,$ $\\, 0.9987 \\,$ $\\, 3.1 \\,$ $\\, 0.9990 \\,$ $\\, 0.9991 \\,$ $\\, 0.9991 \\,$ $\\, 3.2 \\,$ $\\, 0.9993 \\,$ $\\, 0.9993 \\,$ $\\, 0.9994 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p)$, if\n\n- $np > 5$, and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$,\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nthat is,\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p))\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.47$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nnp &= 50 \\cdot 0.47 \\\\\n&= 23.5 \\\\\n&> 5✓ \\\\\nn(1 - p) &= 50 \\cdot (1 - 0.47) \\\\\n&= 26.5 \\\\\n&> 5.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (50)(0.47) = 23.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (50)(0.47)(1-0.47) = 12.455\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(23.5, 12.455)$.\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(29 < X < 35)$. Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(29 < X < 35) = P(30 \\leq X \\leq 34) \\approx P(29.5 \\leq Y \\leq 34.5)\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(29.5 \\le Y < 34.5) &= P(\\frac{29.5 - 23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}} \\le Z \\le \\frac{34.5 - 23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(1.70 \\le Z \\le 3.12) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 3.12) - P(Z < 1.70) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\le 3.12) - P(Z \\le 1.70) \\\\\n&= Φ(3.12) - Φ(1.70) \\\\\n&= 0.9991 - 0.9554 \\\\\n&= 0.0437\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(29 < X < 35) \\approx 0.044\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0436$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1f13b1a-8de4-5337-b09e-91782377760d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114884} {:answer/id #uuid "ea365193-b90a-58ac-a1f2-322193762b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114885} {:answer/id #uuid "1d648d3c-b79a-5e25-8044-84600a900a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114886} {:answer/id #uuid "51c6f36a-e07d-5179-a0e6-ee9c73b96778" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114887} {:answer/id #uuid "7328fd99-74d5-5b0e-af0d-58f247b1149e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114888}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c12a1c0d-d919-5d9c-ba5d-231ad9aadde1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114883}] :question/id #uuid "0991d4ae-bc93-583a-a172-d0ca1666f01a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95696" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\n1. The probability of getting $12$ defective bolts in a box containing $20$ bolts if the probability that an individual bolt is defective is $0.2$.\n2. The probability that a soccer player scores $20$ of $30$ penalties if the probability of scoring each penalty is $0.55$.\n3. The probability of getting $9$ fives on $28$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
